# Tables this gate cannot verify as protected from text alone, as at
# 2026-09-04 — NOT a list of known defects. Every one of them IS under RLS in
# isaflow_dev; migrationRlsRule.ts just can't see it, because migrations 348
# and 498 grant RLS to `company_id` tables DYNAMICALLY (`DO $$ ... FOR r IN
# (SELECT ... FROM pg_policies ...)`), discovering non-compliant tables by
# querying the catalogue at apply time rather than naming any of them as
# literal text. There is nothing for a static scanner to read a table name
# out of in either migration — see migrationRlsRule.ts's own comment for the
# one dynamic shape (505_close_runs.sql's FOREACH/ARRAY/EXECUTE-format loop)
# this gate CAN and does recognize.
#
# So this fails only on a table NOT in this list — a table with no
# ENABLE/FORCE/POLICY the gate can find ANYWHERE, same-file, later-file, or
# 505's array shape, AND not already known-safe via 348/498. That is exactly
# how 595_gl_account_merges.sql was found: it is the one table (as of this
# writing) whose RLS could not be verified either by this gate OR by the
# database-gated integration test, because it genuinely had none — not a
# gate limitation, a real gap. See docs/mcp/FINDINGS.md for the pair of
# findings this gate shipped with.
#
# Delete a line once its table's protection becomes visible as literal text
# (i.e. its own migration, or a later one, explicitly names it in an
# ALTER/CREATE POLICY statement) — the gate then verifies it directly and no
# longer needs the baseline entry. Do NOT add a table to this list because
# the gate is failing on it and you have not confirmed why; confirm first,
# against isaflow_dev, the way 596_gl_account_merges_rls.sql's own commit
# message did.
